#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189089} :activity/id #uuid "4886bded-bc52-550f-be94-ef03345f5d14" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049835 :topic/id #uuid "1799affe-81a7-5a7c-a4ab-d18a27e25076" :topic/math-academy-id 3854} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189061 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189360 :tutorial/content "To find the first derivative of a parametric curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}.\n$$\n\nA formula for the second derivative of a parametric curve can be found by applying the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{dy}{dx}) \\\\  & =\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})\\cdot \\frac{d\\theta }{dx} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see an example of how to apply this." :tutorial/id #uuid "16aecece-e4cd-56e7-bdd9-a19c15eb3136" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1124 :tutorial/title "Calculating Second Derivatives of Polar Curves"} :step/id #uuid "1bec56da-aad1-51b3-af70-e54f081bf7c0" :step/math-academy-id 2211 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189062}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189062 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188262 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188205 :question/id #uuid "366bcb1d-3fe4-5047-8306-bd28935b5431" :question/math-academy-id "e-631"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14b618a5-02d2-5564-a288-3423a873a746" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048867}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373195}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Polar Curve"} :step/id #uuid "b2ee3a4d-b10f-5633-a0b0-9087e7785ae4" :step/math-academy-id 2212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189089 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189368 :tutorial/content "It's often helpful to use the double-angle formula for sine when computing the slope of the tangent to a polar curve, as this often makes the differentiation easier.\n\nLet's consider the polar curve $r = 1-2\\cos\\theta,$ whose graph is shown below \n\n![](images/53/53c660d25a8bec2346cacd95d70fb3f4ee27afe039bd23cbe7affa3cf04eb09a.png)\n\nRecall that the slope of the tangent (in terms of $\\theta$) is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r = 1-2\\cos\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -2{cos}^{2}\\theta , \\\\ y & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the double-angle formula $\\color{black}\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta$ to simplify our expression for $y.$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we need to calculate $x'(\\theta)$ and $y'(\\theta).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -2{cos}^{2}\\theta )^{'} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{'}-(2{cos}^{2}\\theta )^{'} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )-(2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}2\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}(\\theta ) & ={(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta )}^{'} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{'}-{(\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta )}^{'} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the slope of the tangent is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{'}(\\theta )}{{x}^{'}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -2\\text{ }\\,\\operatorname{cos}2\\theta }{2\\text{ }\\,\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "adced1fe-4c40-50d1-a9b4-12fcf213b6e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6236 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "43d1c988-b5bd-5711-8f19-90e40ffc0bcf" :step/math-academy-id 37856 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189090}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189090 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188282 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187986 :question/id #uuid "deaef5cc-99fb-57e5-a6f2-22f17a7f00af" :question/math-academy-id "e-10033"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "111da4c7-612b-5e1f-a105-2dbaaea4e5a5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050277}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320987}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Polar Curves Using the Double-Angle Formula for Sine"} :step/id #uuid "96db70eb-88bd-53e8-beda-04d15d1f59e8" :step/math-academy-id 37857 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189061}}] :activity/title "Further Differentiation of Curves Given in Polar Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186188449}]}
